.TH SMP 9
.SH NAME
smp \- the other cores
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "kernel"
.PP
.nf
.B "init_smp :: proc () -> bool"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kernel/smp.odin
brings up every core the bootloader listed beyond the first, and is the
last thing
.I kmain
does, deliberately: every self-test above it was written for one core and
counts something that a second core would change, so all of them run
before a second core exists to change it.
.SS How a core starts
The bootloader parks every core but the boot core, each spinning on a
word of its own in the machine state the bootloader handed over: its own
page tables, its own stack, interrupts off, and none of the kernel's trap
tables.
.B init_smp
gives each one a kernel stack from the heap, fills in an
.B Ap_Boot
record with that stack, an id and the core's local APIC id, then stores
.B ap_entry's
address into the core's
.B goto_address
atomically, so the core sees the record whole or not at all.
That store is what releases it; nothing else does.
.PP
A released core lands in
.BR ap_entry ,
still on the bootloader's stack and tables.
It turns SSE on, exactly as the boot core's
.B early_init
did, then calls
.B arch.ap_switch
to move onto its own kernel stack and the kernel's own address space,
which is the only way off a stack while still standing on it.
From there
.B ap_main
repeats
.IR kmain 's
early order for this one core: trap tables, the paging bits the
bootloader cleared, the syscall MSRs, the local timer, a scheduler
record, then
.B cpu_online
and
.BR sched.exit .
A core's arrival thread has no future beyond that exit; its stack goes
back to the heap when the idle thread reaps it.
.SS Waiting for the cores
.B init_smp
waits for every released core to report online, bounded by
.B SMP_PATIENCE
ticks.
A core that has not set its
.B online
flag by then is reported missing, and the machine runs on the cores that
did arrive; a one-core machine, or a bootloader with no multiprocessor
feature, is not a fault.
.B --smp=N
to
.IR build (8)
sets how many cores QEMU presents, four by default.
.SS Locks that span cores
.B kernel/sched's
own lock is held across a context switch, not just around the run
queue: a thread being parked or readied is a single operation from the
scheduler lock's own point of view, released only once the incoming
thread's frame is written and nothing on the departing core can read the
outgoing thread's stack again.
An idle core is woken with a kick, an interprocessor interrupt on
.BR VECTOR_WAKE ,
sent when a thread is placed on a core whose current thread is that
core's idle thread; see
.IR sched (9).
A change to one address space's page tables that another core may have
cached is followed by a shootdown, which asks every core running that
space to drop the range and waits for each to count itself off; see
.IR space (9)
for the mechanism and the rule that the sender may hold no spinlock while
it waits.
.PP
The process table's own spinlock,
.B table_lock
in
.BR kernel/user/user.odin ,
guards the table across every core; nothing that can park runs under it,
which is why a closing descriptor group or namespace runs against a
record already marked
.B collecting
under the lock and then released before the parking work begins.
.PP
The kernel log's
.B Logger.lock
is a
.B Spinlock
held for the writes of one line, across every sink and never across
formatting.
The panic path does not take it: it seizes it, because the holder may be
a core the panic just stopped and will never release it.
.SS Stopping the machine
A fault that reaches the panic screen calls
.B arch.ipi_stop_others
first, which sends every other core a non-maskable interrupt.
A core that receives one while
.B panicking
is set halts on the spot, on the NMI's own stack, holding whatever it
held.
An NMI that arrives with the flag clear is hardware reporting something
this kernel does not otherwise handle, and is a panic of its own.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/smp.odin
.br
.B kernel/verify_smp.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR sched (9),
.IR sync (9),
.IR space (9),
.IR arch (9),
.IR build (8)
.SH BUGS
.B MAX_CPUS
is eight.
A machine with more cores than that runs on the first eight the
bootloader lists.
There is no way to bring a core back online once it fails to report; a
missing core stays missing for the life of the boot.
